{"name":"Mountain Green","hex":"#b2b599","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mountain green","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of green earth (“a pigment from the minerals celadonite and glauconite”).","Synonym of Paris green (“a pigment from copper(II) acetate and arsenic trioxide”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountain_green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mountain Green, Utah","blocks":[{"text":"Mountain Green is a census-designated place in northwestern Morgan County, Utah, United States. The population was 4,231 at the 2020 census. Located 16 miles (26 km) up the Weber River from Ogden, Mountain Green is the world headquarters of the Browning Arms Company."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Mountain_Green_Utah_sign.jpeg/330px-Mountain_Green_Utah_sign.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain_Green%2C_Utah","wikidata":"Q9035432"},{"title":"Paris green","blocks":[{"text":"Paris green is an arsenic- and copper-containing pigment. It is an emerald-green crystalline powder that is also known as Mitis green, Schweinfurt green, Sattler green, emerald green, Vienna green, emperor green or mountain green. It is highly toxic and has thus also been used as a rodenticide and insecticide. Its formula is Cu(C2H3O2)2·3Cu(AsO2)2."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Subunit_of_Cu2As3O3%28OAc%29_%28ASOCUA%29.png/330px-Subunit_of_Cu2As3O3%28OAc%29_%28ASOCUA%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris_green","wikidata":"Q339657"},{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg/500px-Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg/1280px-Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg/500px-Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mountain Green Fire District","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Paris_Green_%28Schweinfurter_Gr%C3%BCn%29.JPG/500px-Paris_Green_%28Schweinfurter_Gr%C3%BCn%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Paris_Green_%28Schweinfurter_Gr%C3%BCn%29.JPG/500px-Paris_Green_%28Schweinfurter_Gr%C3%BCn%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Can of Paris green pigment by Sherwin-Williams Co.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParis_Green_(Schweinfurter_Gr%C3%BCn).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg/120px-Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg/250px-Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg/120px-Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.